 "title": "The Psychology of Advertising According to Don Draper",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "This clip from Mad Men shows Don Draper explaining advertising psychology to tobacco executives. The creator claims that advertising is based on happiness, which he defines as freedom from fear and reassurance that you are okay.",
 "key_points": [
  "When multiple companies make identical products, you can say anything you want to differentiate them.",
  "Advertising is based on one thing: happiness.",
  "Happiness is the smell of a new car or freedom from fear.",
  "Happiness is a billboard on the side of the road that screams with reassurance that whatever you are doing, you are okay."
 ],
 "content": "Nobody sells an idea quite like Don Draper. Watch Jon Hamm as Don Draper in Season 1 of Mad Men, streaming for free on Xumo Play. [speaker 1] This is the greatest advertising opportunity since the invention of cereal. We have six identical companies making six identical products. We can say anything we want. How do you make your cigarettes? [speaker 2] I don't know. [speaker 1] Shame on you. We breed insect-repellent tobacco seeds, plant them in the North Carolina sunshine, grow it, cut it, cure it, toast it. There you go. [writes on board] Lucky Strike. 'It's toasted.' [speaker 2] But everybody else's tobacco is toasted. [speaker 1] No, everybody else's tobacco is poisonous. Lucky Strike is toasted. [speaker 3] Well gentlemen, I don't think I have to tell you what you just witnessed here. [speaker 2] I think you do. [speaker 3] Advertising is based on one thing. Happiness. And you know what happiness is? Happiness is the smell of a new car. It's freedom from fear. It's a billboard on the side of the road that screams with reassurance that whatever you're doing, you're okay. You are okay. [speaker 2] It's toasted. [speaker 1] I get it. Text shown on screen: This scene perfectly captures the psychology of advertising.",
